================================================================================
Opengrep taint A/B - ARM ON (taint analysis enabled) - console and result record
harness/artifacts/logs/taint-ab-on.log
================================================================================

================================================================================
WHAT THIS FILE ESTABLISHES
================================================================================

  The taint-ON arm of the controlled A/B that asks whether Opengrep's taint
  engine is active on Spark's own Scala. It carries the arm's exact command, the
  engine and ruleset identity it ran under, its exit code, its wall time, the
  finding it produced with the line and the dataflow trace attached to it, and
  its console output verbatim.

  An A/B proves a capability only through the CONTRAST between two invocations
  that differ in one setting. A log reading 'taint enabled, one finding at line
  72' proves nothing on its own - the finding could be a pattern match. So this
  file also carries the control arm this run measured alongside it, and it
  reports the contrast as it came out rather than as it was expected to.

  It does NOT record 'taint: enabled' from a flag or from documentation and call
  that proof. AAP 0.1.3 is explicit: enabled-in-config is not proof.

================================================================================
WHY THIS FILE IS UNDER logs/ AND NEVER UNDER raw/
================================================================================

  Two independent reasons, either of which is sufficient (AAP 0.5.1, 0.6.1):

  1. raw/ is RUNNER-ONLY. It receives exactly one artifact per tool that writes
     one and nothing else, ever. This arm's SARIF is named
     harness/artifacts/logs/taint-ab-on.sarif for that reason: an A/B artifact
     landing in raw/ could overwrite the real opengrep.sarif that the Stage 3
     runner writes ($HARNESS_RAW_DIR/opengrep.sarif), and the dataset would then
     be built from an A/B over one file instead of a scan over the whole scope.

  2. This is one of the run's TWO DELIBERATE SECOND APPEARANCES. Opengrep
     appears here as the taint A/B subject and again as one of the nine scanned
     runners in Stage 3; Joern appears as a runner and again as the capability
     probe's subject. In each case the second appearance writes outside raw/ and
     contributes NO dataset row. Folding either into findings.json would corrupt
     both that tool's count and the dataset total (AAP 0.3.2), and reading the
     double appearance as a duplication would corrupt both counts (AAP 0.1.1).

  PHASE 6 turns both reasons into checks rather than leaving them as intentions.

