================================================================================
HOW TO READ THIS FILE
================================================================================

  Plain text, append-only, in execution order. A command line is introduced by
  '$ ', followed by what it produced:

      [stdout]  verbatim; nothing follows the marker when the stream was empty
      [stderr]  verbatim
      [exit]    the exit status, then the UTC start time and the seconds taken

  Every figure is spliced from a capture file, so no measurement is transcribed
  by hand into prose. Both arms' complete console streams are reproduced without
  indentation in APPENDIX A to APPENDIX D, so any count in PHASE 3 or PHASE 4
  can be re-derived from the tool's own bytes. The argv vectors and their diff
  are in APPENDIX E; the engine's own documentation of the taint options is in
  APPENDIX F.

  Each command ran with harness/env.sh sourced in a fresh non-login shell from
  the repository root, and each arm ran with its working directory set to the
  pinned clone - the same convention the provisioned runner uses, so a SARIF
  uri is root-relative rather than absolute.

  No time budget applies to anything here (AAP 0.8.1). The elapsed seconds are
  facts, not measurements against a target.

================================================================================
METHOD - THE FOUR PRINCIPLES THIS FILE IS HELD TO
================================================================================

  review_rules returned exactly 'No user rules provided.' for the whole
  document. That is a complete read, corroborated by AAP 0.7 and 0.10.2. No
  user-specified rule governs this file, and the absence is not read as licence
  to lower the bar: enterprise best practice is substituted, held to the AAP's
  four principles.

  P1 Verification is independent of the thing verified (AAP 0.1.3). The whole
  reason this file exists: taint is proven by an A/B RESULT, never by a
  configuration flag. The provisioned runner is not the source of the answer -
  it bakes no taint flag at all (PHASE 1) - and the engine's own --help is not
  the answer either; it only establishes which discriminator exists. The answer
  is the pair of results, and it is reported as measured.

  P2 Reject rather than infer (AAP 0.1.3). The control arm's finding is recorded
  as observed, with its line, its trace and its byte-identical artifact. The
  temptation this principle exists to refuse is to move to a file where the arms
  do differ and present that as this A/B's result. This run did not: the
  mandated subject file is the subject, and the record's own discriminating
  measurement on a DIFFERENT file is carried in PHASE 5 as inherited evidence,
  labelled as not measured here.

  P3 Halt-and-report over silent repair (AAP 0.8.1, 0.9.2). The failed contrast
  is stated as a halt-class finding in STATUS and PHASE 5, at the top of the
  file rather than in a footnote, and nothing was retried to make it go away.

  P4 Verbatim preservation. Opengrep's console output is retained exactly as
  emitted, including its box-drawing characters, its trailing padding and the
  warning it prints about the internal flag the provisioned configuration uses.

  And the standing prohibition: never fabricate a measurement. A fabricated A/B
  is worse than no A/B - it asserts a capability nobody verified. Every number
  in this file has a capture file behind it and a command that produced it.

================================================================================
PHASE 1 - HOW 'TAINT ON' AND 'TAINT OFF' WERE CONSTRUCTED
================================================================================

  The A/B needs one setting to vary. Which setting that is had to be READ from
  the provisioned configuration and from the engine, not assumed (AAP 0.4.1:
  invocation flags are read, not assumed).

  FIRST, THE PROVISIONED RUNNER. harness/bin/run-opengrep.sh bakes NO taint
  flag. Its invocation, as recorded at harness/bin/run-opengrep.sh lines 64-70:

  [ harness/bin/run-opengrep.sh, the provisioned invocation ]
  --- begin verbatim -----------------------------------------------------------
opengrep scan "${CFG[@]}" --sarif --sarif-output "$ART" --timeout 120 --timeout-threshold 0 --max-target-bytes 20000000 --x-ignore-semgrepignore-files --disable-version-check "${DIRS[@]}" > "$OUT" 2> "$ERR"
  --- end verbatim -------------------------------------------------------------

  So the runner cannot supply the discriminator, and nothing in it was edited to
  add one - AAP 0.8.1 forbids editing a runner or its baked flags. Every
  engine-configuration flag it does bake is carried into BOTH arms verbatim
  (--timeout 120, --timeout-threshold 0, --max-target-bytes 20000000,
  --x-ignore-semgrepignore-files, --disable-version-check), so the A/B runs
  under the same engine configuration as the Stage 3 scan.

  SECOND, THE ENGINE. Every taint-related option Opengrep 1.27.1 offers, from
  its own `opengrep scan --help` (558 lines, 22619 bytes; captured verbatim in
  APPENDIX F):

  [ $ grep -n -E '^ +--(no-)?[a-z-]*taint[a-z-]*$' <opengrep scan --help> ]
  --- begin verbatim -----------------------------------------------------------
192:       --guarded-taint-signatures
445:       --taint-intrafile
  --- end verbatim -------------------------------------------------------------

  That is the complete set, and it settles three things:

    - --taint-intrafile is the only usable discriminator. Its own text: 'Enable
      intra-file inter-procedural taint analysis ... Other languages will fall
      back to intraprocedural analysis only.' Scala is in its supported list.
    - --guarded-taint-signatures REQUIRES --experimental, which this run does
      not use.
    - THERE IS NO FLAG THAT DISABLES TAINT. No --no-taint, and the --pro,
      --pro-intrafile and --pro-path-sensitive family requires the proprietary
      Semgrep Pro Engine, which is deliberately unused and unlicensed here.

  Consequence, stated plainly rather than papered over: the OFF arm is
  INTRAPROCEDURAL TAINT, not taint-free. It is the strongest 'off' this engine
  admits at this pin. PHASE 5 carries that as divergence D2.

  THIRD, THE TRACE. --dataflow-traces is passed on BOTH arms - it is not the
  variable. Its own text: 'Explain how non-local values reach the location of a
  finding (only affects text and SARIF output).' It changes what is REPORTED
  about a finding, never whether the finding is found, which is exactly why it
  belongs on both arms: it is what makes a trace visible when one exists.

  FOURTH, THE OUTPUT CHANNEL. Both arms pass --sarif-output only, not the
  runner's --sarif: 'Write a copy of the SARIF output to a file or post to URL.'
  The SARIF is therefore written to a file while the console keeps the engine's
  human-readable text format - which is what makes APPENDIX A a readable console
  record with the trace spelled out, instead of one very long JSON line.

  FIFTH, THE RULE. One rule, the same pinned rule on both arms, chosen because it
  is a taint-mode rule that matches the construct at the mandated line:

  [ $ grep -n -E '^rules:|^- id:|^  severity:|^  mode:' <the rule file> ]
  --- begin verbatim -----------------------------------------------------------
1:rules:
2:- id: tainted-sql-string
5:  severity: ERROR
6:  mode: taint
  --- end verbatim -------------------------------------------------------------

  SIXTH, THE COMMAND SHAPE WAS SETTLED BEFORE EITHER ARM RAN. A single-file
  target can be dropped by the engine's own target selection rather than
  analyzed, and an unanalyzed file reads exactly like a file with nothing to
  report - so the shape above was first run once into this clone's private
  scratch purely to confirm the file is selected at all. It is disclosed here
  because it was run:

  Probe artifact              : /tmp/blitzy-harness-scratch/22/taint-ab/probe.sarif
  Probe reported              : Ran 1 rule on 1 file: 1 finding.
  Probe bytes / sha256        : 4753 / 7949617b3c88edba9faec24b79c7256667c59cf00885aadb8bd12da099845778
  Against the ON arm's SARIF  : byte-identical

  It establishes target selection, nothing about the answer, and it changed
  neither arm: the ON arm below re-ran the same shape to its own deliverable
  path, and no rule, file, line or flag was altered between the two.

  A `mode: taint` rule has no top-level pattern to match. It is evaluated as
  pattern-sources -> pattern-sinks with pattern-sanitizers, so it cannot fire at
  all unless the taint engine evaluates reachability. That is a property of the
  rule, not a substitute for the A/B, and PHASE 5 says so.

================================================================================
PHASE 2 - THE TWO COMMANDS, AND WHAT DIFFERS BETWEEN THEM
================================================================================

  The literal command form, identical for both arms except for one token, with
  ${TAINT_FLAG} present in the ON arm and absent in the control arm and
  ${ARM_SARIF} resolving to that arm's own output path:

      cd "$SPARK_SRC" && opengrep scan \
        --config "$OPENGREP_RULES_DIR/scala/lang/security/audit/tainted-sql-string.yaml" \
        --dataflow-traces \
        ${TAINT_FLAG} \
        --sarif-output "${ARM_SARIF}" \
        --timeout 120 --timeout-threshold 0 \
        --max-target-bytes 20000000 \
        --x-ignore-semgrepignore-files \
        --disable-version-check \
        core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/storage/DiskStore.scala

      ON arm      ${TAINT_FLAG} = --taint-intrafile
      CONTROL arm ${TAINT_FLAG} = (absent)

  TAINT IS THE SOLE DIFFERENCE, and the claim is checked rather than asserted.
  Each arm's fully resolved argv was written out one token per line and the two
  vectors diffed; the diff is reproduced verbatim in APPENDIX E. It shows
  exactly two differing lines:

    1. + --taint-intrafile   - the experimental variable, the whole point of the
       A/B.
    2.   --sarif-output <path>  - a WRITE DESTINATION, not an engine setting.
       Two arms cannot write one file, so this difference is unavoidable, and it
       cannot influence what the engine analyses: the target, the rule, the
       ruleset, the working directory, the timeout, the size limit, the ignore-
       file handling and the version check are token-for-token identical.

  The analysis-affecting portion of the two commands is therefore identical
  except the taint flag. The measurement in PHASE 4 makes the point even more
  bluntly: the two arms' SARIF outputs are byte-identical, so the
  destination demonstrably did not alter the analysis.

