================================================================================
PHASE 5 - DIVERGENCES, EACH REPORTED AND NONE REPAIRED
================================================================================

  D1  HALT-CLASS. The A/B pair does not discriminate on the mandated subject.

      Expected (AAP 0.5.1, restated in 0.9.1): 'one traced finding at line 72
      with taint on and zero with it off, from two invocations differing only in
      that setting'. Observed: 1 at line 72 with the flag, 1 at line 72 without it,
      artifacts byte-identical. The ON half holds; the OFF half does not.

      Disposition: AAP 0.9.2 lists 'a failed taint A/B' among the conditions that
      STOP the run. This file records it as the halt-class finding it is. It is
      not repaired here, and nothing downstream should read the ON arm's single
      traced finding as a proven contrast, because there is no contrast.

      WHY THE ARMS CANNOT DIFFER ON THIS FILE - the mechanical reason, from the
      evidence in PHASE 1 and PHASE 3 rather than from speculation. The rule's
      source is a method parameter and its sink is the interpolated string. Here
      both sit inside ONE method: the parameter is declared at line 64 (`def
      put(blockId: BlockId)`) and the sink is at line 72, and the trace this arm
      attached confirms it - step 0 is `$blockId` at line 72 column 21, step 1
      is the sink at line 72 column 13. The flow never crosses a method
      boundary, so INTRA-FILE INTER-PROCEDURAL taint - which is precisely and
      only what --taint-intrafile adds - has nothing to contribute. The default
      intraprocedural taint analysis already reaches it.

      This explains the observation; it does not excuse it. The expectation
      anchored to this file remains unmet, and that is what STATUS says.

  D2  Engine limit: a taint-FREE arm is not constructible at this pin.

      PHASE 1 establishes it from the engine's own option list: the only taint
      options are --taint-intrafile and --guarded-taint-signatures (the latter
      requiring --experimental), and the --pro family requires the proprietary
      Semgrep Pro Engine. Nothing disables taint. So 'taint off' in this A/B
      means 'intraprocedural taint' rather than 'no taint', and a reader must not
      take the OFF arm as a pattern-matching-only control.

      Recorded rather than worked around: reaching for --experimental or a Pro
      engine to manufacture a taint-free arm would change the flag set to obtain
      an expected answer, which AAP 0.1.3 forbids, and the Pro engine is
      unlicensed and deliberately unused (AAP 0.3.2).

  D3  Ruleset identity: no divergence. Observed equals expected, so no
      not-comparable marking is warranted for this tool on this ground.

  Ruleset, observed           : commit f1d2b562b414783763fd02a6ed2736eaed622efa
  Ruleset, expected           : commit f1d2b562b414783763fd02a6ed2736eaed622efa
  Identity matches            : yes
  Rule count, observed        : 2006 rules
