      report 1 finding at line 72, which the record itself calls
      NON-DISCRIMINATING - and it is why provisioning moved its own A/B to a file
      where the arms do differ. This run's observation agrees with the record on
      that point, so under AAP 0.1.3's authority rule both are recorded and the
      run continues past the agreement itself; what does not resolve is the
      expected-values anchor in D1, which the table carries and the record does
      not override.

      The record additionally reports a DISCRIMINATING pair on a different file -
      sql/core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/jdbc/JdbcDialects.scala, 12
      findings with --taint-intrafile against 11 without it, lines 659, 666, 670
      and 676 reachable only with the flag, all 12 carrying codeFlows. That
      measurement is INHERITED and UNANCHORED: the expected-values table names no
      such subject, and this run did NOT re-run it. Doing so would be retrying on
      a different file to obtain the expected answer, which is the one thing P2
      forbids. It is recorded because a fact this file could not establish must be
      named rather than omitted - not as a substitute for the mandated A/B.

  WHAT THE ON ARM DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH ON ITS OWN - stated explicitly, so
  no reader has to infer it:

      DOES: the taint engine evaluated this file. The finding came from a
      `mode: taint` rule, which has no top-level pattern and is evaluated only as
      source-to-sink reachability, and the result carries a codeFlows trace whose
      steps name the source and the sink. A pattern match emits no codeFlow.

      DOES NOT: the CONTRAST that would make that conclusion independent of how
      the engine loads and evaluates rules. AAP 0.1.3 requires the difference in
      result between two invocations, and on this subject that difference is zero.
      This file therefore claims what it measured and nothing beyond it.

